.TH RUN-APPAUDIT 1 "2026-11-25" "cti-agent" "CTI Agent Fleet"
.SH NAME
run-appaudit \- was every web application scanned, and did its owner get a report?
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-appaudit
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs
.B cti\-appscan \-\-audit
over the last 8 days and writes the result for the check-in to read:
.TP
.I $FLEET_HOME/reports/appscan\-audit.txt
every web application the scanner defines, one line each: its id, name,
owner, newest scan and a verdict, the ones that need a look first;
.TP
.I $FLEET_HOME/reports/appscan\-audit.json
the same, with each application's
.B kind
code, for the check-in.
.PP
An application with no scan in the window is the case nothing else shows:
the weekly AppSec report and the owners lane both start from the scans that
ran. The check-in escalates those once, by name, and tracks them on the
board.
.PP
It runs as the appscan lane, with that lane's credentials
.RB ( cti\-agent\-appaudit.service ),
because the audit needs the scanner's login and reads the mailbox, and the
check-in's unit holds neither. The check-in only reads the files.
.PP
Read-only: the scanner and the mailbox are read, the owners lane's delivered
log is read, and nothing is sent, filed or recorded. It writes only the two
reports, 0600, atomically. An audit that cannot run (the scanner
unreachable, say) is written to the JSON as
.B error
with the reason, for the check-in to escalate once.
.SH OPTIONS
None. It takes no mode: it changes nothing but its own reports.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the reports were written, whatever they say, including an audit that
could not run; 2 for any argument; otherwise 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo cti\-agent run\-appaudit
sudo cat /var/lib/cti\-agent/reports/appscan\-audit.txt
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-appscan (1),
.BR run\-appowners (1),
.BR run\-credcheck (1)
